    "items": [
      {
        "text": "Michael E. Gates is a former trial attorney who served as Huntington Beach's city attorney for a decade. He previously worked in the private sector as an attorney representing trucking companies. In 2025, he was appointed a deputy assistant attorney general in the U.S. Department of Justice's Civil Rights Division, a job he left in November to return to the Huntington Beach city attorney's office.",
        "cites": [1, 2, 3],
        "sentences": [
          {
            "text": "Michael E. Gates is a former trial attorney who served as Huntington Beach's city attorney for a decade.",
            "cites": [1]
          },
          {
            "text": "He previously worked in the private sector as an attorney representing trucking companies.",
            "cites": [2]
          },
          {
            "text": "In 2025, he was appointed a deputy assistant attorney general in the U.S. Department of Justice's Civil Rights Division, a job he left in November to return to the Huntington Beach city attorney's office.",
            "cites": [3]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "text": "His candidate statement says public safety is his top priority, and he supports enforcing Proposition 36 and prosecuting all crimes. He says he supports law enforcement and would bring programs he credits with lowering crime and homelessness in Huntington Beach to cities across California. He adds that he would prosecute fraud and corruption in Sacramento, protect parents' rights, enforce laws keeping boys out of girls' sports, and support voter ID. As city attorney, he fought parts of new state housing laws that would have made Huntington Beach build more affordable housing.",
        "cites": [4, 3],
        "sentences": [
          {
            "text": "His candidate statement says public safety is his top priority, and he supports enforcing Proposition 36 and prosecuting all crimes.",
            "cites": [4]
          },
          {
            "text": "He says he supports law enforcement and would bring programs he credits with lowering crime and homelessness in Huntington Beach to cities across California.",
            "cites": [4]
          },
          {
            "text": "He adds that he would prosecute fraud and corruption in Sacramento, protect parents' rights, enforce laws keeping boys out of girls' sports, and support voter ID.",
            "cites": [4]
          },
          {
            "text": "As city attorney, he fought parts of new state housing laws that would have made Huntington Beach build more affordable housing.",
            "cites": [3]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "text": "As city attorney, he helped establish Huntington Beach's in-house community prosecutor program, which prosecuted petty crimes like public drunkenness and trespassing instead of relying on county prosecutors. He says the program reduced crime by double digits.",
        "cites": [5, 4],
